          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "According to the National diabetes association standards of care summary (*Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management*), metformin is the usual starting drug for type 2 diabetes when there is no other major condition that changes the choice. It is described as the usual initial agent, used together with structured lifestyle changes. The guidance gives reasons: long experience with it, low cost, little weight gain, and a low risk of low blood sugar on its own.\n\nThe same summary notes that kidney function is checked before starting and from time to time afterward, because the agent is limited or avoided when kidney function is low.\n\nThe guidance also says the first choice depends on other conditions a person may have, so it is not the same for everyone. The retrieved passages did not describe those other conditions or the alternative first-line options, so I can't tell you what would apply in a particular case. A clinician can explain which approach fits a specific situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
